CAT(1)                         Funny CLI Manual                         CAT(1)

NAME
    cat — Print the contents of a file, because opening an editor
          would be far too sophisticated.

SYNOPSIS
    cat <file>

DESCRIPTION
    The 'cat' command reads the specified file and prints its entire
    contents directly to the terminal.

    It accepts a file path, resolves it to an absolute path, opens it
    as UTF-8 text, and dumps everything onto your screen.

    Everything.

    There is no pagination. There is no "Are you sure?". There is only
    the file and the terminal, locked in an intimate relationship.

ARGUMENTS
    file
        The file to read and print.

        Required. If omitted, 'cat' refuses to guess what you meant
        and politely reminds you how commands work.

ERRORS
    cat error: <error>
        Something went wrong while opening or reading the file.

        Possible causes include:
        - The file does not exist.
        - The path points somewhere unexpected.
        - You lack permission to read it.
        - The filesystem has developed opinions.

USAGE ERRORS
    > cat

        Usage: cat <file>

        You forgot the file.

OUTPUT
    The complete contents of the specified file are printed to stdout.

    Files are opened using UTF-8 encoding. Characters that cannot be
    decoded are replaced rather than causing the command to explode.

    In other words: "I may not understand this character, but I will
    absolutely continue."

EXAMPLES
    > cat README.md
        Prints the entire README.

        Yes, the entire thing. Even the 400-line one.

    > cat config.json
        Displays your configuration.

        A thrilling way to discover what you accidentally committed.

    > cat secrets.txt
        Displays secrets.txt.

        This is probably fine.

        It is not fine.

    > cat missing.txt
        cat error: ...

        The file has chosen to remain mysterious.

FUN FACTS
    - The command is named 'cat', traditionally short for "concatenate",
      despite this implementation mostly just printing one file.
    - UTF-8 is used because it's 2026 and we have standards now.
    - Invalid characters are replaced instead of causing a decoding
      meltdown.
    - File completion is enabled, because manually typing filenames is
      apparently beneath us.
    - Path completion is enabled too, because the filesystem is large
      and your memory is not.

